==> CPU frequency (driver intel_pstate, mode active)
  good: cores ramp to full turbo the moment a request arrives: EPP performance or balance_performance, turbo on, no frequency caps — token generation is a latency-bound loop and every ramp-up shows in time-to-first-token
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/intel_pstate.html#energy-vs-performance-hints, https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/cpufreq.html
  ok:   P-cores 0-15: governor powersave, EPP performance
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{0..15}/cpufreq/energy_performance_preference
  ok:   P-cores 0-15: no frequency cap (hardware max 5100 5300 MHz), min 800 MHz
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{0..15}/cpufreq/{scaling,cpuinfo}_max_freq
  ok:   E-cores 16-31: governor powersave, EPP performance
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{16..31}/cpufreq/energy_performance_preference
  ok:   E-cores 16-31: no frequency cap (hardware max 3900 MHz), min 800 MHz
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{16..31}/cpufreq/{scaling,cpuinfo}_max_freq
  note: power-profiles-daemon is running: it sets the governor and EPP itself, so an EPP or governor write here lasts only until its next profile change
        from:   /proc/*/comm
        why:    power-profiles-daemon's README (https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/upower/power-profiles-daemon, 'Operations on Intel-based machines'): on intel_pstate in active mode it applies the selected profile through the governor and the energy-performance hints. Either keep it and pick its performance profile (powerprofilesctl set performance), or stop it for a benchmark run so EPP writes stick
        apply:  sudo systemctl stop power-profiles-daemon
        revert: sudo systemctl start power-profiles-daemon
  ok:   turbo enabled (no_turbo=0)
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/no_turbo
  ok:   max_perf_pct=100, min_perf_pct=16
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/max_perf_pct
  note: hwp_dynamic_boost=0
        from:   /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/hwp_dynamic_boost
        why:    when 1, a task waking from I/O wait gets a short min-P-state boost; it can help request handlers that block on the network or disk. Unmeasured for inference here — benchmark before keeping it
        apply:  echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/hwp_dynamic_boost
        revert: echo 0 | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/hwp_dynamic_boost
==> CPU idle states (intel_idle, intel_idle.max_cstate=9)
  good: for latency-critical serving, no idle state whose exit latency is large next to a token step; for throughput or power budgets, leave them all on
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/cpuidle.html#representation-of-idle-states, https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/intel_idle.html
  note: enabled idle states with >=100 us exit latency: C2_ACPI/127us C3_ACPI/1048us
        from:   /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/state*/{name,latency,disable}
        why:    a core woken from these pays that latency before it runs; disabling them trims tail latency at the cost of idle power. The 100 us cut-off is this tool's heuristic, not a kernel rule. Runtime disable needs no reboot (intel_idle.max_cstate does)
        apply:  echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state{2,3}/disable
        revert: echo 0 | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state{2,3}/disable
==> CPU thermal throttling
  good: no throttle time under load: the package stays below its TCC activation point (TjMax minus the TCC offset), so cores keep the frequency they request
  src:  kernel source drivers/thermal/intel/therm_throt.c (thermal_throttle/*), drivers/thermal/intel/int340x_thermal/processor_thermal_device.c (tcc_offset_degree_celsius), drivers/hwmon/coretemp.c (tempN_crit = TjMax), Intel SDM vol. 4 335592-093 (IA32_THERM_STATUS 19CH, IA32_PACKAGE_THERM_STATUS 1B1H, MSR_CORE_PERF_LIMIT_REASONS 64FH in table 2-39)
  info: TCC activation 95 C (TjMax 100 C - TCC offset 5 C); package now 95 C
        from: /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4/temp1_{crit,input}, /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:04.0/tcc_offset_degree_celsius
  warn: package thermally throttled 4% of uptime: 22190052 events, 101915027 ms in total, longest 417 ms; core events peak at 8502475 on cpu8
        from:   /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/thermal_throttle/package_throttle_{count,total_time_ms,max_time_ms}, /proc/uptime
        why:    therm_throt counts each time the package raised its thermal or PROCHOT status; while it is set the cores run below the frequency they request, so sustained inference speed follows the cooler, not the clock settings. The 1%-of-uptime cut-off is this tool's heuristic
        apply:  none on the host: improve cooling (airflow, heatsink, BIOS fan or thermal profile); the host-side lever is a lower PL1, see CPU package power limits
        revert: none
  info: MSR 0x1B1 IA32_PACKAGE_THERM_STATUS 0x000000008805080b: now thermal; logged thermal, PROCHOT, power limit; 5 C below TCC activation
        from: /dev/cpu/0/msr (root)
  info: MSR 0x19C IA32_THERM_STATUS (CPU 0) 0x00000000880b2a88: now none; logged PROCHOT, threshold #1, threshold #2, power limit, current limit; 11 C below TCC activation
        from: /dev/cpu/0/msr (root)
  info: MSR 0x64F MSR_CORE_PERF_LIMIT_REASONS 0x0000000019030002: now thermal; logged PROCHOT, thermal, other (electrical), PL2, max turbo limit
        from: /dev/cpu/0/msr (root)
  info: the log bits are sticky: the SDM says they stay set until software writes 0, so they cover the time since boot or the last clear. doctor --perf only reads them
        from: Intel SDM vol. 4 335592-093 (R/WC0 log bits)
==> CPU package power limits (RAPL)
  good: PL1 (long_term) at or above the thermal spec power (TDP, reported as constraint_0_max_power_uw) so sustained inference is not clamped below spec; PL1 decides the clock under a long run, PL2 only covers short bursts. On a package that thermally throttles, a PL1 far above TDP only moves the limit from power to temperature
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/power/powercap/powercap.html, kernel source drivers/powercap/intel_rapl_common.c (PL1 max_power = PKG_POWER_INFO thermal spec power)
  warn: intel-rapl:0 package-0: PL1 117 W is 3.3x the 35 W thermal spec power while the package is thermally throttled 4% of uptime (long_term 117 W, short_term 117 W, peak_power 230 W)
        from:   /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:0/constraint_0_{power_limit_uw,max_power_uw}, /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/thermal_throttle/package_throttle_total_time_ms
        why:    PL1 is the sustained package power. Set above what the cooler removes, it never binds: the package runs into its TCC point and throttles on temperature instead (see CPU thermal throttling). A PL1 the cooler can hold trades peak clocks for steady ones. 2x the thermal spec power is this tool's first step for a sweep, not an Intel figure: measure tokens/s at each step before keeping one. Firmware may re-apply its own value on reboot
        apply:  echo 70000000 | sudo tee /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:0/constraint_0_power_limit_uw
        revert: echo 117000000 | sudo tee /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:0/constraint_0_power_limit_uw
  info: intel-rapl-mmio:0 package-0: PL1 not enabled on this interface (enabled=0)
        from: /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl-mmio:0/enabled
==> Transparent huge pages
  good: enabled = always or madvise (fewer TLB misses over multi-GB weight and KV buffers), defrag != always (no allocation stalls mid-request)
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html#global-thp-controls
  ok:   THP enabled=always
        from: /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled
  ok:   THP defrag=madvise
        from: /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/defrag
  note: root filesystem is ext4 on Linux 6.6.128-1-MANJARO: model files mmapped from it are mapped with 4 KiB pages, whatever THP enabled says
        from:   /proc/mounts, /proc/sys/kernel/osrelease
        why:    on this kernel ext4 page cache holds no large folios (fs/ext4/inode.c calls mapping_set_large_folios from 6.16), and khugepaged collapses file pages only in executable mappings (include/linux/huge_mm.h file_thp_enabled needs VM_EXEC). OpenVINO and llama.cpp mmap weights by default, so THP covers only anonymous copies. A tmpfs with huge=within_size serves the weights from huge pages (docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html, hugepages in tmpfs/shmem); copy the model in and point the server at it
        apply:  sudo mkdir -p /mnt/k3d-gpu-models && sudo mount -t tmpfs -o huge=within_size,size=<model GiB + 1>G tmpfs /mnt/k3d-gpu-models
        revert: sudo umount /mnt/k3d-gpu-models
==> Reserved huge pages (hugetlbfs)
  good: 0 unless a server is configured to use hugetlbfs — reserved pages cannot be used for anything else, and OpenVINO, PyTorch and llama.cpp do not use them by default
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.html
  ok:   HugePages_Total=0, free 0
        from: /proc/meminfo
==> Memory and swap
  good: the model plus KV cache fits in MemAvailable with headroom and nothing is swapped; swapped-out buffers fault back in at random-I/O speed
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.html#swappiness, https://docs.openvino.ai/2026/openvino-workflow/running-inference/optimize-inference/optimizing-latency.html
  info: MemTotal 125.4 GiB, MemAvailable 61.2 GiB, swap 187.5 GiB, vm.swappiness=60
        from: /proc/meminfo
  warn: 107.6 GiB of swap in use — the host is under memory pressure
        from:   /proc/meminfo
        why:    anything a server keeps in anonymous memory (KV cache, runtime buffers, weights it copied) is paged back from disk when touched. Free memory or serve a smaller model before benchmarking; MemAvailable is too small to pull the swapped pages back in right now
  info: pass --model-gb <size> (or K3D_GPU_MODEL_GB) to check a model against MemAvailable
        from: /proc/meminfo
==> DRAM speed, bandwidth and ECC
  good: modules at the platform's plan-of-record (POR) speed for how the slots are populated; single-stream CPU and iGPU decode is bound by DRAM bandwidth. Memory errors counted by an EDAC driver
  src:  SMBIOS DSP0134 7.18 (type 17, as dmidecode.c decodes it), Intel datasheet 743844 rev. 015 table 25 (13th/14th Gen Core DDR support matrix, https://cdrdv2.intel.com/v1/dl/getContent/743844), https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/edac.html
  info: 4x DDR5 SODIMM 32 GiB 2R, ECC (80/64-bit): rated 4800 MT/s, configured 3600 MT/s; 4 of 4 slots populated
        from: /sys/firmware/dmi/entries/17-*/raw (root)
  info: SODIMMs at 2 slots per channel are not a supported configuration on the 13th Gen Core S line (743844 table 25 note 9); the nearest POR row, UDIMM 2DPC, 2 DIMMs 2R, is 3600 MT/s
        from: /proc/cpuinfo (model 13S), /sys/firmware/dmi/entries/17-*/raw
  ok:   configured 3600 MT/s is at the 3600 MT/s POR for 2DPC, 2 DIMMs 2R
        from: /sys/firmware/dmi/entries/17-*/raw (root)
  info: at one module per channel these modules would run at 4800 MT/s (the lower of rated 4800 and the 1DPC 2R POR): 76.8 GB/s peak, with 2 of the 4 modules (less capacity)
        from: 743844 table 25
  info: peak DRAM bandwidth 57.6 GB/s = 2 channels x 3600 MT/s x 8 B (computed; CPU and iGPU decode share it)
        from: /sys/firmware/dmi/entries/17-*/raw (root)
  note: ECC modules, but no EDAC memory controller is registered: corrected and uncorrected errors are not counted (dram.ecc=unmonitored)
        from:   /sys/devices/system/edac/mc/ (no mc*), /sys/firmware/dmi/entries/17-*/raw
        why:    without an EDAC driver a failing module shows up only as crashes or silent corruption; with one, ce_count/ue_count give early warning
        apply:  boot Linux 6.16 or newer (ie31200_edac binds host bridge 8086:a700 from 6.16), then: sudo modprobe ie31200_edac
        revert: sudo modprobe -r ie31200_edac
==> Kernel samepage merging (KSM)
  good: off (run=0, the kernel default) on a node that runs other tenants' pods: merged pages are shared copy-on-write, and the write fault on a merged page is a timing signal another tenant can observe
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/ksm.html, Suzaki et al., Memory Deduplication as a Threat to the Guest OS, EuroSec 2011
  ok:   KSM off (run=0, pages_sharing=0)
        from: /sys/kernel/mm/ksm/run
==> IRQ balancing
  good: irqbalance running, so interrupt load (NIC, NVMe, GPU) is spread over CPUs instead of piling onto the cores that serve tokens
  src:  https://github.com/Irqbalance/irqbalance
  note: irqbalance is not running
        from:   /proc/*/comm, /etc/systemd/system/*.wants/irqbalance.service
        why:    irqbalance spreads the highest-volume interrupt sources across CPUs. It matters most on a busy NIC or many NVMe queues; a single-user desktop sees little difference
        apply:  sudo systemctl enable --now irqbalance   # install the irqbalance package first if the unit is missing
        revert: sudo systemctl disable --now irqbalance
==> GPU PCIe links and BAR sizes
  good: each GPU's link trains at the device's own maximum (its upstream port is not slower or narrower), and the large BAR maps all of VRAM (Resizable BAR)
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/PCI/sysfs-pci.html, kernel source drivers/pci/pci-sysfs.c (max_link_*, resourceN_resize) and drivers/pci/pci.c pci_rebar_get_possible_sizes (bit 0 = 1 MB)
  ok:   0000:01:00.0 [10de:27b0]: Gen4 x16 behind port 0000:00:01.0 (Gen5 x16): the link can reach the device maximum
        from: /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/max_link_{speed,width}, /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:01.0/max_link_{speed,width}
  ok:   0000:01:00.0 [10de:27b0]: BAR1 32 GiB, the largest size it supports
        from: /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/resource, /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/resource1_resize
  warn: 0000:02:00.0 [10de:28b0]: device Gen4 x8, upstream port 0000:00:06.0 Gen4 x4: the link trains at most Gen4 x4
        from:   /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:02:00.0/max_link_{speed,width}, /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:06.0/max_link_{speed,width}
        why:    a link trains to the lower speed and width of its two ends. current_link_* reads lower at idle (link power management), so only max_* is compared. Keep this GPU's weights and KV cache resident (nothing streamed over the link) and give the widest link to the GPU that moves the most data
        apply:  hardware: move the card to a slot whose port is at least x8 (no host command changes the link width)
        revert: hardware: move it back
  ok:   0000:02:00.0 [10de:28b0]: BAR1 16 GiB, the largest size it supports
        from: /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:02:00.0/resource, /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:02:00.0/resource1_resize
  info: GPU peer-to-peer not checked: nvidia-smi not found
        from: nvidia-smi topo -p2p r
==> Intel GPU frequency
  good: max request at RP0 (no cap); boost at RP0 so a waiting client gets full clock; under GuC SLPC the firmware picks the frequency between min and max
  src:  kernel source drivers/gpu/drm/i915/gt/intel_gt_sysfs_pm.c + uc/intel_guc_slpc.c + intel_gt_pm_debugfs.c (i915: RP1 floor, waitboost counters), https://docs.kernel.org/gpu/xe/xe_gt_freq.html (xe)
  info: card0 i915 gt0: GuC SLPC; cur 1650 / act 0 MHz, min 300, max 1650, boost 1650, RPn 300, RP1 700, RP0 1650
        from: /sys/class/drm/card0/gt/gt0/rps_*_freq_mhz
  info: card0 gt0: slpc_ignore_eff_freq=0, so GuC SLPC keeps the efficient frequency RP1 700 MHz as its floor while busy, above min 300 MHz (i915 drops the floor to min only when the flag is 1)
        from: /sys/class/drm/card0/gt/gt0/slpc_ignore_eff_freq, /sys/class/drm/card0/gt/gt0/rps_RP1_freq_mhz
  info: card0 gt0: waitboost count not readable (debugfs needs root: sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/gt0/uc/guc_slpc_info)
        from: /sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/gt0/uc/guc_slpc_info
  note: card0 gt0: min 300 MHz — the GPU ramps from there on each burst
        from:   /sys/class/drm/card0/gt/gt0/rps_min_freq_mhz
        why:    pinning min to RP0 removes the ramp for bursty single-user inference but holds full clock (and power) while idle; on a shared-TDP part it also takes power budget from the CPU cores. Benchmark both
        apply:  echo 1650 | sudo tee /sys/class/drm/card0/gt/gt0/rps_min_freq_mhz
        revert: echo 300 | sudo tee /sys/class/drm/card0/gt/gt0/rps_min_freq_mhz
  info: HuC: not readable (debugfs needs root: sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/dri/*/gt0/uc/huc_info) — HuC is the HEVC/AVC media microcontroller, not used by inference
        from: /sys/kernel/debug/dri/*/gt0/uc/huc_info (see https://docs.kernel.org/gpu/i915.html#huc)
==> Isolation for sandboxed agents
  good: /dev/kvm usable for microVM sandboxes, each GPU alone in its IOMMU group, split-lock detection on, CET known, GPU observation streams limited to CAP_PERFMON
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/vfio.html (IOMMU groups), https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/kernel-parameters.html (split_lock_detect), https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/kernel.html (split_lock_mitigate), kernel source drivers/gpu/drm/i915/i915_perf.c and drivers/gpu/drm/xe/xe_observation.c (paranoid sysctls), Intel SDM vol. 4 335592-093 (IA32_S_CET 6A2H, IA32_TME_ACTIVATE 982H)
  info: /dev/kvm present; kvm_intel nested=Y enable_apicv=Y ept=Y: microVM sandboxes (Kata, Firecracker) can run here
        from: /dev/kvm, /sys/module/kvm_intel/parameters/*
  ok:   0000:00:02.0: alone in IOMMU group 0 (with its own functions and upstream ports): can be passed to a microVM by itself
        from: /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:02.0/iommu_group/devices/
  ok:   0000:01:00.0: alone in IOMMU group 2 (with its own functions and upstream ports): can be passed to a microVM by itself
        from: /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/iommu_group/devices/
  ok:   0000:02:00.0: alone in IOMMU group 4 (with its own functions and upstream ports): can be passed to a microVM by itself
        from: /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:02:00.0/iommu_group/devices/
  info: 2 IOMMU unit(s); default domain type: identity
        from: /sys/class/iommu/, /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/*/type
  info: CET flags: user_shstk, ibt; IA32_S_CET 0x0000000000000004: kernel IBT on; user shadow stacks are opt-in per process (glibc.cpu.x86_shstk tunable)
        from: /proc/cpuinfo, /dev/cpu/0/msr (root)
  ok:   split-lock detection warn (default), split_lock_mitigate=1
        from: /proc/cpuinfo, /proc/cmdline, /proc/sys/kernel/split_lock_mitigate
  info: TME capable but off (IA32_TME_ACTIVATE 0x0000000000000001: lock=1 enable=0; mem-encryption=tme-capable-off). Enabling is a BIOS setting
        from: /dev/cpu/0/msr (root)
  ok:   dev.i915.perf_stream_paranoid=1
        from: /proc/sys/dev/i915/perf_stream_paranoid

==> 11 suggestion(s). Read-only: nothing on this host was changed.
